1	# NoHelperMethodInTestClassRule
2	
3	| Property | Value |
4	|----------|-------|
5	| Identifier | `customRules.testClassNonOverrideMethod` |
6	| Scope | Test classes in restricted namespaces |
7	| Configurable | Yes (namespace prefixes) |
8	
9	## What It Detects
10	
11	Reports methods in test classes that are not:
12	
13	- **Test methods**: methods starting with `test` or annotated with `#[Test]`
14	- **Data providers**: methods starting with `provider`
15	- **Framework overrides**: methods that override a parent class method (abstract implementations or `#[Override]`-annotated overrides)
16	
17	```php
18	namespace Tests\Unit\Service;
19	
20	final class UserServiceTest extends TestCase
21	{
22	    public function testCreate(): void { ... }
23	
24	    public function providerInvalidEmails(): array { ... }
25	
26	    #[Override]
27	    protected function setUp(): void { ... }
28	
29	    // ERROR: Move method buildUser() out of Tests\Unit\Service\UserServiceTest or make it a test, data provider, or framework override.
30	    protected function buildUser(string $name = 'Alice'): User
31	    {
32	        return new User($name, 'alice@example.com', 30);
33	    }
34	
35	    // ERROR: Move method assertUserIsValid() out of Tests\Unit\Service\UserServiceTest or make it a test, data provider, or framework override.
36	    protected function assertUserIsValid(User $user): void
37	    {
38	        self::assertNotEmpty($user->name());
39	        self::assertNotEmpty($user->email());
40	    }
41	}
42	```
43	
44	## Why This Is an Error
45	
46	Test classes should have a flat, predictable structure: test methods, data providers, and framework lifecycle overrides. Any other method is a helper that introduces problems:
47	
48	1. **Shared abstractions reduce readability**: A test should be a self-contained specification. When setup or assertions are hidden behind helper methods, the reader must navigate multiple methods to understand one test case.
49	
50	2. **Helpers grow into frameworks**: Helper methods tend to accumulate parameters, default values, and conditional logic over time. Eventually the test class becomes a mini-framework that requires its own documentation.
51	
52	3. **Hides what each test actually needs**: A `buildUser()` helper with defaults obscures which parameters matter for a specific test case. Inline construction makes each test's requirements explicit.
53	
54	## How to Fix
55	
56	### Option 1: Inline into test methods
57	
58	```php
59	final class UserServiceTest extends TestCase
60	{
61	    public function testCreate(): void
62	    {
63	        $user = new User('Alice', 'alice@example.com', 30);
64	        $service = new UserService();
65	
66	        $service->create($user);
67	
68	        self::assertNotEmpty($user->name());
69	        self::assertNotEmpty($user->email());
70	    }
71	}
72	```
73	
74	### Option 2: Use a real library boundary
75	
76	For genuinely reusable setup logic, use an existing library or create an independent internal library outside the `Tests` namespace. Give the class a domain-specific API instead of placing it in a generic support/helper namespace:
77	
78	```php
79	namespace Acme\TestingFixtures\User;
80	
81	final class ExampleUser
82	{
83	    public static function alice(): User
84	    {
85	        return new User('Alice', 'alice@example.com', 30);
86	    }
87	}
88	```
89	
90	```php
91	// tests/Unit/Service/UserServiceTest.php
92	final class UserServiceTest extends TestCase
93	{
94	    public function testCreate(): void
95	    {
96	        $user = ExampleUser::alice();
97	        // ...
98	    }
